*Context: *On February 24, 2026, OpenRouter released data that showed the
top three most popular models on its platform were all Chinese models:
MiniMax M2.5, Moonshot AI=E2=80=99s Kimi K2.5, and Zhipu GLM-5. Favored by
individual developers and AI start-ups, OpenRouter is an API aggregation
platform that gives access to different AI models from dozens of companies.
<https://substack.com/redirect/e8a372b9-75fa-4354-a81e-2b8cfccb6e7a?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>
Source:
OpenRouter leaderboard from March 1, 2026.

This week=E2=80=99s feature article (link to *original Chinese
<https://substack.com/redirect/af882688-0b3c-461e-b266-414d3017a413?j=3DeyJ=
1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*),
from the TechFlow [=E6=B7=B1=E6=BD=AE] WeChat public account, explores the =
implications of
American developers integrating Chinese open-source models. Here=E2=80=99s =
how this
process maps onto physical infrastructure:

*An American developer in San Francisco sends an API request. Data travels
from California, via an undersea fiber optic cable in the Pacific, to a
data center somewhere in China. The GPU cluster starts working, electricity
flows from China=E2=80=99s power grid to the chips, inference is completed,=
 and the
results are sent back. The entire process may only take a second or two.*

*Key Takeaways: Why have Chinese models become so popular with indie
developers and researchers on OpenRouter? According to TechFlow, the simple
answer is price-to-performance ratio:*

*On OpenRouter, Chinese developed large-scale model MiniMax M2.5 scored
80.2% on the software engineering task, while Claude Opus 4.6 scored 80.8%,
a negligible difference. However, the prices are vastly different: the
former costs $0.30 per million tokens, while the latter costs $5 (per
million tokens), a difference of approximately 17 times.*


   -

   This is due to China=E2=80=99s cheaper electricity prices (40% lower tha=
n in the
   US) as well as algorithmic advantages of some Chinese models: =E2=80=9C*=
DeepSeek
   V3=E2=80=99s MoE architecture activates only a subset of parameters duri=
ng
   inference. Independent tests show that its inference cost is about 36 ti=
mes
   lower than GPT-4o*=E2=80=9D.
   -

   Sensitivity to cost has increased due to the emergence of OpenClaw, an
   open-source autonomous AI agent created by software developer Peter
   Steinberger. For a time, before the two companies cracked down, develope=
rs
   used their monthly unlimited credits to connect their OpenClaw agent wit=
h
   their Anthropic and Google subscription accounts. Now, some of these
   developers have turned toward Chinese models. Tech Flow cites a comment
   from OpenRouter COO Chris Clark: the reason Chinese open-source models h=
ave
   captured a large market share is because they are =E2=80=9C*disproportio=
nately
   heavy in agentic flows run by U.S. firms*.=E2=80=9D

*In contrast to most reporting that paints U.S.-China tech decoupling as an
all-consuming narrative; this piece illustrates continued interconnections
that might not show up in the numbers:*

*=E2=80=9CTokens have no physical form, do not go through customs, are not =
subject
to tariffs, and are not even included in any current trade statistics.
China exports a significant amount of computing power and electricity
services, but this is almost invisible in official commodity trade data.=E2=
=80=9D*


   -

   One interesting parallel is to Chinese =E2=80=9Celectricity exports=E2=
=80=9D in the
   earlier wave of Bitcoinc mining, which converted Chinese cmoputing power
   into digital assets.
   -

   The author even goes as far as to compare what is happening with the
   export of Chinese tokens =E2=80=94 cut-throat competition among Chinese =
companies
   to drive down token prices =E2=80=94 to a =E2=80=9CMade in China=E2=80=
=9D export strategy.

*That said, I think this piece does point out a trend to watch, it likely
overstates the significance of OpenRouter data. *TechFlow hypes up the
geopolitical stakes:

*Whoever=E2=80=99s model becomes the default infrastructure option for glob=
al
developers gains, implicitly, a structural influence on the global digital
economy. This is precisely what truly unsettles Washington about the global
expansion of Chinese tokens.*

*When a developer=E2=80=99s codebase, agent workflow, and product logic are=
 all
built around an API based on a particular Chinese model, migration costs
will increase exponentially over time. At that point, even if the US
legislates restrictions, developers will resist with their feet, just as no
programmer can abandon GitHub today.*


   -

   However, as the article admits, U.S. companies will likely be hesitant
   to use platforms like OpenRouter to leverage Chinese models: =E2=80=9C*A=
n API
   request from an American developer is processed through a Chinese data
   center, physically passing through China. This isn=E2=80=99t a problem f=
or
   individual developers and small applications, but it=E2=80=99s a fatal f=
law in
   scenarios involving sensitive corporate data, financial information, and
   government compliance. This is why the Chinese model has the highest
   penetration rate in development tools and personal applications, but alm=
ost
   no presence in core enterprise systems.*=E2=80=9D
   -

   In a previous ChinAI issue, I also noted that OpenRouter usage
   represents only a small slice of the AI ecosystem: For reference, in
   November 2025, users consumed *663B tokens from MiniMax
   <https://substack.com/redirect/069c900a-0126-4a16-8dd0-0bb5231892d3?j=3D=
eyJ1IjoiMmZseDYifQ.6O_iC1_GWCzChnbrHzSrs1hMpJpoODTUJm_FAk3tf6E>*
   over the past month on the OpenRouter platform. In their July earnings
   report, Google processed 980T tokens monthly. As one helpful Reddit
   commentator noted (h/t to PackAccomplished5777): =E2=80=9C*OpenRouter is=
 a drop
   in the bucket, most people (and companies) still use the native provider=
s +
   things like Azure OpenAI, Google Vertex, Amazon Bedrock*.=E2=80=9D

